# 01 — Business Understanding
## World Vision Australia — Donor Retention Analytics

**Business question:** How can WVA use first-gift information to inform who to prioritise, when to test re-engagement and how to make follow-up more relevant?

A client-based capstone at Deakin University, using client-supplied data and mentor support to develop evidence-informed retention recommendations.

## Business Findings at a Glance

The final analysis examined repeat one-off giving within **24 months**.

| Finding | Business implication |
|---|---|
| **16.6%** of 19,194 eligible supporters made a repeat gift. | Progression deserves investigation, while recognising that other forms of ongoing support fall outside this outcome. |
| The model’s **Top 20% captured 33.7%** of repeat donors in the test cohort. | Ranking provides a basis for testing differentiated retention attention. |
| **75.7% of observed repeat donors** returned within 12 months. | The first year provides candidate windows for re-engagement tests. |
| Retail Stand Internal recorded **3.7%** repeat giving versus **17.0%** for Website. | The Retail Stand journey warrants investigation before deciding on changes. |

*The 16.6% rate describes the broader analytical cohort. Model results use a separate Mass test cohort of 6,741 supporters, with a 13.8% baseline. Timing describes supporters who repeated within 24 months. Channel comparisons describe the broader analytical cohort.*

These findings identify opportunities to investigate and test. They do not establish that additional contact will cause more donations.

## 1. The Business Challenge

A first one-off gift creates an opportunity to develop the supporter relationship. WVA needs to understand which supporters give again, when they return and which first-gift characteristics could inform retention decisions.

A one-off donor may already have a relationship with WVA through previous sponsorship or regular giving. Treating every first observed one-off donor as newly acquired would overlook that context.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A["First qualifying giving occasion"] --> B["Another qualifying one-off gift within 24 months"]
    A --> C["No qualifying repeat observed within 24 months"]
    C --> D["May still engage or support WVA in other ways"]
```

The analysis measures one specific progression outcome. It does not measure the full value of every supporter relationship.

## 2. Decisions the Evidence Supports

| Decision | Evidence used | Practical next step |
|---|---|---|
| **Who should receive additional attention?** | Repeat-propensity ranking | Test additional care within priority groups |
| **What could make communication more relevant?** | First-gift product, amount, channel and prior relationship | Test context-based messages against standard communication |
| **When should re-engagement be tested?** | Second-gift timing | Compare selected first-year or anniversary contact windows |
| **Which journey needs investigation?** | Channel differences within product context | Audit Retail Stand onboarding and contact quality |

These are proposed uses for donor retention, fundraising, CRM and analytics teams. Operational ownership and feasibility require confirmation.

## 3. What the Project Measures

| Definition | Business rule |
|---|---|
| **Unit of analysis** | One supporter |
| **First giving occasion** | Earliest observed qualifying gift date; same-day transactions form one occasion |
| **Repeat outcome** | Another qualifying gift on a later date within 24 months |
| **Follow-up** | A complete 24-month observation window |
| **Eligibility** | Exclude identified BounceBack transactions and supporters with active ongoing commitments at the first occasion; retain eligible former ongoing supporters |
| **Model population** | WVA-classified Mass acquisition supporters |
| **Prediction point** | End of the first giving occasion, using information available by then |

The broader descriptive cohort and predictive population are distinct. Their rates must retain their own population labels.

Campaign execution, production deployment, causal attribution and lifetime-value prediction are outside the completed project scope.

## 4. From Analytical Evidence to Business Value

**The completed analysis provides:**
- A defined progression measure.
- Evidence about supporter patterns and timing.
- An evaluation of historical prioritisation.
- Recommendations for controlled testing.

**A future pilot must establish whether those recommendations create value.**

| Measure | What it answers |
|---|---|
| Repeat rate, lift and capture | Does the ranking concentrate observed repeat donors? |
| Treatment–control conversion difference | Does the activity generate additional repeat giving? |
| Donation value per allocated supporter, compared with contact costs | Is the additional activity economically worthwhile? |
| Opt-outs and complaints | Is supporter experience being protected? |

A high propensity to repeat does not necessarily mean a supporter will respond more strongly to additional contact. Testing within priority groups helps distinguish these questions.

## 5. Interpretation Boundaries

“First observed” depends on the completeness of the supplied history. Data sources have uneven coverage, and historical patterns may change.

Group differences are associations. Recommendations remain proposed actions, with no implemented retention uplift claimed. Detailed data checks and model-validation limitations are documented in the following stages.

